      <p class="kicker">Distinction</p>
      <h1>Surety Bonds vs. Insurance</h1>
      <p>Insurance pays you for a covered loss. A surety bond pays someone else if you fail the obligation, and you reimburse the surety.</p>
      <AuthorBio />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The core difference</h2>
      <p>Insurance protects the policyholder. A surety bond protects the obligee, and the principal remains responsible for a paid claim.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://surety.org/surety-fidelity/what-is-surety/">Surety &amp; Fidelity Association of America</a> defines a surety bond as a three-party agreement: the surety guarantees the obligee that the principal will perform.</p>
      <p><strong>Insurance pays you.</strong> A fire, a lawsuit, or a crash is a loss the policy is written to cover. You are the one the policy pays.</p>
      <p><strong>A surety bond pays the obligee.</strong> If the principal misses the regulation, the project, or the court order, the surety pays the harmed party and then collects from the principal.</p>

      <h2>Who pays after a claim</h2>
      <p>On an insurance claim, the insurer pays the covered loss. On a surety claim, the principal reimburses the surety for what the surety paid.</p>
      <p>Renewal pricing can change after an insurance claim. A paid surety claim is a debt under the indemnity agreement.</p>

      <h2>Why credit prices a bond</h2>
      <p>Surety pricing follows the chance the principal will reimburse a paid claim, so credit is the main input on a standard license bond.</p>
      <p>Insurance pricing follows how often that kind of loss happens. The <a href="/guides/credit-score-impact/">credit guide</a> shows the bands this site’s sketch uses.</p>

      <h2>When a business needs both</h2>
      <p>A contractor usually needs liability insurance for jobsite injury and a surety bond for the license or the contract. They pay different parties.</p>
      <p>A <a href="/bonds/collection-agency/">collection agency</a> can need professional liability insurance and a collection-agency surety bond at the same time. One does not replace the other.</p>

      <h2>What “bonded and insured” means</h2>
      <p>“Bonded and insured” means the business has a surety bond and a liability policy. They are separate contracts, often from separate companies.</p>

      <p>Sketch a bond on the <a href="/#calculator">calculator</a> or <a href="/contact/">call the preferred desk</a>.</p>
      <p>If the next question is which company should sign the bond, use the <a href="/guides/best-surety-bond-companies/">best surety bond companies shortlist</a>.</p>
    </div>
